cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "Exact Hypergraph Duality for Multi-Rate Lattice Sampling under Common-Mode Drift"
abstract: >-
  A periodic system with period T is sampled by grids, the divisors of T above
  one, and each grid seats the divisors of T in blocks of section classes. This
  package computes, in exact rational arithmetic, the maximum phase margin any
  design of a given period can attain (a closed-form function of T, not a
  constant), the cheapest design attaining a requested margin (an exact
  hypergraph coverage problem), and the fewest shared sections that hold that
  margin. Margins above the instance ceiling are reported as infeasible at any
  cost, by duality rather than by search failure. A margin kept under a common
  drift is priced in the same currency -- keeping v under a drift of d costs
  exactly what keeping v + d costs with none -- and the reach a design has above
  its witness margin is proved unspendable, so a service level is one purchase
  and the drift is paid out of the instance ceiling. A set of service lengths
  generates a period -- their least common multiple -- so a schedule already in
  production can be priced on its own lattice instead of a period chosen by
  example. The universe of a period is read two ways, by the grids and by the
  divisors, and the two agree: a divisor is carried exactly when some even grid
  dividing the period seats it, so membership is a filter over divisors rather
  than a seat map over grids. The same reading turns the scaled copies of a
  fixed block into a law: whether k times a block lands in a universe is decided
  by the carrier grids of its parts and one least common multiple, with no
  period swept, so the periods at which scaled designs clear the free line are
  named rather than searched. Carrying is also a two-sided window on the
  cofactor: whatever seats a divisor a as a median has a cofactor between
  2 (a - mass(a)) / a and 2 Phi(a) / a, read off a alone, so which divisors are
  no grid's median at all is decided with no grid built and no horizon named --
  and the bound is attained, so it cannot be lowered. Two readers therefore
  disagree in two distinguishable ways, a window that misses a cofactor being a
  theorem failing and a grid limit being short being a measurement short, and
  the package reports them separately rather than as one disagreement.
